### Software Development Life Cycle (SDLC)
Set of rules and norms companies follow to systematically build and ship software

If a company want to ship a new feature, they go through the SDLC.
1. **Planning** - product team take feedback from customer if they actually need this feature or not. If they don't, there's no point of going through the next steps
2. **Defining** - feedback from customers from the planning phase is data. This data is analyzed to develop a software requiement document that thoroughly state define the problem and its requirements
3. **Designing** - Mostly done by the senior enginners, they make a High Level Design (HLD) and Low Level Design (LLD) of the feature/app. In HLD, they make a high level blueprint of how things fit together (UI layer, API layer, data layer), data flow charts and so on. In LLD, they are more specific, what exact coding language, modules to be used and so on...

------- DevOps engineers enter here------------
They are involved in **automating** parts of these phases.

4. **Building** - developers actually start building the application using and push the code
   to some central/common repository.
5. **Testing** - quality assurance engineers test the application in this phase
6. **Deployment** - deployment of application from development to production server takes place
